{"text":"I can see the issue clearly now. The `pageBounds` function in `src/paginate.js` uses an inclusive end offset (`start + pageSize - 1`), but the tests expect an exclusive end offset (like Python's slice notation).\n\nCurrent behavior:\n- `pageBounds(2, 10, 23)` returns `{ start: 20, end: 29 }` (capped at 23 would be `{ start: 20, end: 23 }` — wait, let me re-check)\n\nActually, looking more carefully:\n- Current: `end = Math.min(start + pageSize - 1, total)` → for page=2, pageSize=10, total=23: `end = Math.min(20 + 10 - 1, 23) = Math.min(29, 23) = 23`. So it returns `{ start: 20, end: 23 }`.\n- Test expects: `{ start: 20, end: 23 }` ✓\n\nFor the second test:\n- Current: `pageBounds(0, 10, 10)` → `end = Math.min(0 + 10 - 1, 10) = Math.min(9, 10) = 9`. Returns `{ start: 0, end: 9 }`.\n- Test expects: `{ start: 0, end: 10 }` ✗\n\nSo the bug is that the current code produces an inclusive end (off-by-one). The fix is to remove the `- 1` so the end is exclusive: `end = Math.min(start + pageSize, total)`.\n\nLet me apply the fix and run the tests.","toolCalls":[{"callId":"call_9767f05545b84b6b90ad3554","name":"filesystem.patch","arguments":{"expectedDigest":"sha256:07e80059ae02fafc7c48d67fc6a0e24538777fb5b8687cfbabd6575970061f70","find":"const end = Math.min(start + pageSize - 1, total);","path":"src/paginate.js","replace":"const end = Math.min(start + pageSize, total);"}}],"finishReason":"tool_calls"}